      "failure_scenario": "TCS actually reported accelerating revenue growth of ~16-17% in FY2022 driven by post-COVID digital transformation demand surge, meaning the 4.6% assumption dramatically understates actual performance and anchors all three forecast years to a structurally unrepresentative base period distorted by pandemic headwinds",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "Net income margin expansion is capped by using a 3.2% adjusted PAT growth rate, implying margins remain broadly stable and no significant operating leverage or cost pressures emerge",
      "failure_scenario": "A sharp rise in employee attrition forces TCS to increase wages and hiring costs substantially, or INR appreciation compresses dollar-denominated revenue realisation, causing net income growth to diverge materially from the 3.2% trajectory and breaking the assumed stable margin structure",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "The share count remains static at FY2021 weighted average shares outstanding of approximately 374.01 crore shares for all three forecast years, with no buybacks or dilution",
      "failure_scenario": "TCS executes one or more share buyback programmes (as it has historically done repeatedly), reducing the share count and causing actual EPS to be materially higher than forecast, making the EPS projections an underestimate and undermining their usefulness for valuation multiples",
      "risk": "Medium"
    }
  ],
  "confidence_score": 18,
  "overall_critique": "This forecast is critically flawed by its over-reliance on a single anomalous pandemic-affected year as the sole basis for growth rate extrapolation, ignoring multi-year historical averages, management guidance, and visible demand tailwinds that rendered the 4.6% revenue growth assumption a severe underestimate from the outset. The mechanical straight-line projection methodology with no scenario sensitivity, no macro adjustments, and a frozen share count produces a false sense of precision while masking fundamental uncertainty about the trajectory of the world's third-largest IT services company."
}
